Z||000009||pHoOl||09-18-2001||01:50 PM||||Sometimes i wonder about people who go that fast when they don't know what they're doing.  My roommate just said about how he got his bike up to either 120 or 140 on the freeway from Detroit to Grand Rapids.  I could not imagine going that fast.  Just nuts<P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>        <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||MinnGreenGT||09-18-2001||01:57 PM||||I've had my GT up to (and a little past) 120mph. But never in traffic, never with anyone else around. I've had it up to about 100mph or 110mph with my wife in the car, but she's OK with it.  I don't ever really "Street Race" like you'd see in a movie (The Fast & The Furious... good flick BTW). Moreso I just have fun with other good-natured drivers traveling at "Above-the-limit-speeds"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>EDIT: It's all fun and games until someone gets an eye poked out...<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfpole.gif">   <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett<p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 09-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||OcalaFiero||09-18-2001||02:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>I've had my GT up to (and a little past) 120mph. But never in traffic, never with anyone else around. I've had it up to about 100mph or 110mph with my wife in the car, but she's OK with it.  I don't ever really "Street Race" like you'd see in a movie (The Fast & The Furious... good flick BTW). Moreso I just have fun with other good-natured drivers traveling at "Above-the-limit-speeds"    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>That's the difference between you (& me) and these 18-20 year old kids that daddy just bought a new car for. At that age they think their invincible, well, bridge abutments and armco barriers are alot stronger than they are. There were a couple of morons down in Orlando a few months back, 1 in a Lambo (no, a real one) and 1 in an Acura (I think). Needless to say there were some bodies and twisted car parts and not much else left afterwards.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>Ocala, Florida<BR>'86 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||09-18-2001||04:38 PM||||I like going very fast too. I raced for real. But I do mine on the hiway, out in the boonies, no one else around. And I dont get in over my head. Lots of people just go fast and have no perception of what there doing. How many of you got passed by a 60 yo woman in an SUV going 95 MPH. I guarantee, anything goes wrong and she wouldnt have a clue.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mach10||09-18-2001||06:33 PM||||That's just it. It's a feeling of invincibility, coupled with the adrenaline rush of speed. I don't travel faster than about 90mph, EVER. The roads in my part of the world are FAR too treacherous. Bumpy, saggy, and washboard, all in the same 10m stretch. The Fiero feels fine at those speeds, but I still won't venture much past. I did once in my FWD rice-rocket Mazda, and it didn't feel good, at all. Chalk that up to inexperience perhaps. But then, I don't think I'm any less experienced than these folks...<BR>The key is to know your limits, and don't push them. Highway Kms do not count as high-speed driving experience, as these young people found out in a hurry. I'm taking some courses next year. Should be fun, breaking 100mph in a CLOSED COURSE...||||reg||3
Z||000014||Wolfhound||09-18-2001||06:55 PM||||Here is the story, not pretty. Racing is for the race track!<BR> <A HREF="http://www.dispatch.com/news-story.php?story=dispatch/news/news01/sep01/850117.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dispatch.com/news-story.php?story=dispatch/news/news01/sep01/850117.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||MinnGreenGT||09-19-2001||02:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by OcalaFiero:<BR><B><P>That's the difference between you (& me) and these 18-20 year old kids that daddy just bought a new car for. At that age they think their invincible, <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No offense taken of given (I hope), but I'm a little young for your reference  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I'm 22 and my Wife is 20 (Married for a year last Saturday).   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>But we're often referred to as "old" or "mature" for our age (homeowner, business owner, married, etc...)  I learned my "invincible" lesson when I was 17.  I was driving my (parent-donated) Suzuki Samuari home from church the day after an ice storm.  And guess what!  The tranny locked one wheel (was going about 45mph) and slid the car sideways into the ditch.  I rolled it 2.5 times landing it on the roof against a tree at the edge of a frozen lake.  The passenger side "rollbar" (POS) broke and collapsed... crushing where my Girlfriend normally would have been riding!  This certainly changed my outlook on driving... so I do consider myself "older" -especially when comparing to others close to my age that act like they're 16 and race without a mind or knowelege of their car!<P>well, that's my rant... so anyone who meets me- remember, I'm not that punk kid you see flying around town.  I'm just like you: an automotive enthusiast.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfpole.gif">  <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000016||OcalaFiero||09-19-2001||03:42 PM||||No offense taken at all, we all know who the "problem children" in the world are. There are people my age (32..arrrggh) and older who are less mature behind the wheel than some 16 year olds. I'm not painting with a broad brush here, not all younger drivers are bad. It just takes them learning that they can't drive it like a racecar on the roads at all times.  I learned my lesson at 16, doing 75-90 on back roads late one evening (in my mother's Olds Delta 88..ecch). Luckily I was pulled over by a police officer that didn't give even me a ticket, but I learned more from that incident than any other, just seeing those blue lights and wiondering what the heck I was going to tell my parents scared the heck out of me.<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>Ocala, Florida<BR>'86 2M4<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smallsigpic_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
